// Arm a transfer of `nbytes` bytes in direction `dir` and pulse it into motion.
//
// completes_when: SPI_LEN and SPI_CTRL.dir are programmed and SPI_CTRL.start
//                 has been written. The transfer is RUNNING, not finished.
// waits for:      nothing -- this is the arm half. wait_done() is the other.
//
// Splitting arm from wait is what lets both transfer styles share this code.
// A programmed transfer has to interleave FIFO accesses between the two;
// a DMA transfer has nothing to do in between at all.
//
// TWO WRITES, IN THIS ORDER, and it matters. `dir` decides which way the shift
// engine sources data, so it has to be settled before the pulse; folding both
// into one write_fields() would coalesce them into a single bus
// read-modify-write and lose the ordering (§21.14.1). write_field() also names
// the field to the compiler, so a typo is a compile error rather than a wrong
// bit.
import spi_regs_pkg::*;
import spi_types_pkg::*;

extend component spi_c {

    target function void start_transfer(spi_dir_e dir, bit[32] nbytes) {
        regs.len.write_val(nbytes);
        regs.ctrl.write_field("dir", (bit[32])dir);
        regs.ctrl.write_field("start", 1);
    }
}
